---
import PostLayout from "../../layouts/PostLayout.astro";

const content = `
<p>
  A well-organized documentation site header is essential for navigation. If you are using the
  ReadTheDocs theme and need to add custom menu items (product links, related docs, dropdown menus),
  you can dynamically inject them using JavaScript.
</p>

<h2>The Approach</h2>

<p>
  Inject custom HTML elements into the page header on load. This avoids modifying the Sphinx theme
  source code directly and keeps customizations maintainable across theme updates.
</p>

<pre><code>document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
  var customHeaderCode = \\\`
    &lt;div class="header"&gt;
      &lt;div class="header-right"&gt;
        &lt;a href="/index.html"&gt;Documentation Home&lt;/a&gt;
        &lt;div class="dropdown"&gt;
          &lt;button class="dropbtn"&gt;Related Documents&lt;/button&gt;
          &lt;div class="dropdown-content"&gt;
            &lt;a href="/getting_started/index.html"&gt;Getting Started&lt;/a&gt;
            &lt;a href="/getting_started/faqs.html"&gt;FAQs&lt;/a&gt;
          &lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  \\\`;

  var customHeaderDiv = document.createElement('div');
  customHeaderDiv.innerHTML = customHeaderCode;
  document.body.insertBefore(customHeaderDiv, document.body.firstChild);
});</code></pre>

<h2>How to Use</h2>

<ol>
  <li>Save the JavaScript file in your <code>_static/js</code> folder in your reST source directory</li>
  <li>Reference it in your <code>conf.py</code> so Sphinx includes it in the build</li>
  <li>Build the documentation site with <code>sphinx-build</code></li>
</ol>

<h2>How It Works</h2>

<ul>
  <li>The script waits for the DOM to fully load before executing</li>
  <li>It creates a new <code>div</code> element containing the custom header HTML</li>
  <li>The element is inserted at the top of the document body, appearing above all other content</li>
  <li>Links, dropdowns, and styling are fully customizable within the template string</li>
</ul>

<h2>When to Use This</h2>

<p>
  This approach works well when you need cross-product navigation links in a documentation site
  that serves multiple products or product versions. It keeps the navigation consistent without
  requiring theme modifications that would break on updates.
</p>
`;
---

<PostLayout
  title="Adding Custom Menu Items to Your Documentation Site Header"
  date="2023"
  summary="How to inject custom navigation menus into ReadTheDocs-themed documentation sites using JavaScript."
>
  <Fragment set:html={content} />
</PostLayout>
